::JOUR_U01_L01_GP_Q01::[MLA.JOUR.ETH.01] Scenario: The principal confirms that the media lab opens Monday at 8:00 a.m. Students need quick factual information. Which format best fits this need? {
~A. Feature profile#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
=B. News brief#Correct. This answer applies the lesson standard to journalism practice.
~C. Editorial#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~D. Photo essay#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
}
::JOUR_U01_L01_GP_Q02::[MLA.JOUR.ETH.01] Scenario: A student is writing a news brief about bus route changes. Which quality expectation is most important for the format? {
~A. Invent a dramatic opening scene#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~B. Include only the writer opinion#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
=C. State verified who, what, when, where, and source information#Correct. This answer applies the lesson standard to journalism practice.
~D. Use jokes to make the story entertaining#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
}
::JOUR_U01_L01_GP_Q03::[MLA.JOUR.NEWS.06] Which sentence uses journalism vocabulary most accurately? {
=A. The audience is the group the journalist is trying to inform.#Correct. This answer applies the lesson standard to journalism practice.
~B. A format is any rumor that sounds interesting.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~C. Publication-ready means the writer likes the story.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~D. News judgment means choosing the longest article every time.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
}
::JOUR_U01_L01_GP_Q04::[MLA.JOUR.MED.09] Scenario: A school newspaper once printed monthly, but now posts updates online during the week. What does this show? {
=A. Technology can change journalism timing and publication format.#Correct. This answer applies the lesson standard to journalism practice.
~B. Journalism no longer needs verification.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~C. Digital posts are always editorials.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~D. Print journalism was never factual.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
}
::JOUR_U01_L01_GP_Q05::[MLA.JOUR.ETH.01] Scenario: An editorial claims the school should add a recycling program but gives no evidence. What is the best judgment? {
~A. It meets editorial standards because opinions never need support.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~B. It should become a feature story instead of being revised.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~C. It should be published only if the headline is catchy.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
=D. It needs credible evidence because editorials must support claims.#Correct. This answer applies the lesson standard to journalism practice.
}
